+\textbf{Quantization comparison is two models, two quantization
+implementations, with opposite outcomes.} The Hunyuan-MT-7B result
+(Section~\ref{sec:modelselect}) is a Q4\_K\_M GGUF quantization of a 7B
+model that lost more than 12 BLEU points relative to its full-precision
+reputation; the MiLMMT-46-4B result (Section~\ref{sec:milmmt4b}) is a
+4-bit bitsandbytes NF4 quantization of a 4B model that \emph{gained}
+relative to a larger float16 competitor. Having both outcomes in the same
+study is useful precisely because it forecloses a general claim in either
+direction: 4-bit quantization does not reliably cost $>$12 BLEU points, nor
+does it reliably preserve quality. Different quantization schemes (GGUF
+Q4\_K\_M vs.\ bitsandbytes NF4), different base architectures, and
+different amounts of quantization-aware fine-tuning in the base model's
+own training are all known in the broader quantization literature to
+produce divergent outcomes~\cite{dettmers2023qlora}. Our claim remains
+narrow and specific to each measurement: for a given model, at a given
+quantization scheme, on this task, neither the full-precision reputation
+nor the bit-width alone predicted the quantized, on-device result, which
+is sufficient to justify measuring rather than assuming --- in either
+direction --- for any specific deployment decision.
 
